## A caveat before we start

This lesson is a guide, and its text is dated **30 October 2026**. Data protection law changed in recent years (the UK’s Data (Use and Access) Act 2025 amended the UK GDPR, and US states keep adding laws) and it will change again. It is general information, not legal advice. It explains what to ask, not what to answer: for a real site a lawyer checks the final texts and the order of actions, and you read the current wording of each law yourself through the links at the end. Where an article is named below, you can find it in the text of the law. Where it says “in practice,” that is an interpretation which is common today, not the letter of the law. For questions about your own site, ask a data protection lawyer, or the authority itself: the Information Commissioner’s Office (ICO) in the UK, your national data protection authority in the EU, the California Privacy Protection Agency (CPPA) in California.

## Why this is about you

In [the lesson on privacy by design](../../07-data/07-privacy-by-design/) you saw the main thing already: if a site collects a name and a phone number, you are working with **personal data**. Here is the same question from the legal side.

There are three bodies of law in this course for English speakers, and they differ in shape:

- The **GDPR** (the General Data Protection Regulation of the European Union, Regulation 2016/679) is one law for the whole EU, with one set of rules for almost every organization that handles personal data.
- The **UK GDPR**, together with the **Data Protection Act 2018**, is the United Kingdom’s own copy since it left the EU. It is almost the same in structure. The regulator is the ICO.
- The **United States** has no single federal privacy law for ordinary websites. Instead there are laws for particular sectors (health, children, banks) and, now, a patchwork of **state laws**. California’s Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA, as changed by the CPRA) is the best known. By one count, 24 states had a comprehensive privacy law by late October 2026, and counts differ with the date and the source.

The first words of the GDPR (Article 4):

- **Personal data** is any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person. A name, a phone number, an email, an address, a photo, an online identifier. Information about an organization (its name and number) is not personal data, but its employee’s name and work email are.
- **Processing** is any operation performed on personal data: collecting, recording, storing, using, sending, deleting. Just **receiving** an inquiry and keeping it in your inbox is processing.
- A **controller** is the one who, alone or with others, determines the purposes and means of processing. If the form on your site collects names, you are the controller, whether you are a sole trader, a freelancer or a company. The law does not apply to purely personal or household activity (an address book of friends), but a site with a customer form is not that.
- A **data subject** is the person whose data is processed: your visitor.
- A **processor** is whoever processes personal data on behalf of the controller: a form service, an email provider, a CRM. You remain responsible to the person.

## Does it apply to me?

It depends on **whom you serve**, not only on where you sit.

- The **GDPR** applies if you are established in the EU, and also, under Article 3(2), if you are outside the EU but offer goods or services to people in the EU (payment or not) or monitor their behavior there. A site that a few Europeans happen to open does not become subject to the GDPR for that reason alone. A site with prices in euros, shipping to EU countries and a language chosen for them does look like “offering.”
- The **UK GDPR** works the same way for the UK.
- The **CCPA** applies to for-profit businesses doing business in California that meet any one of three tests: annual gross revenue above $26,625,000 (the figure adjusted for inflation from 1 January 2025), buying, selling or sharing the personal information of 100,000 or more California residents or households, or earning 50 percent or more of annual revenue from selling or sharing it. Many small sites fall below all three. Other states have thresholds of their own, usually by the number of residents whose data you process.

A small site below every American threshold can still face the GDPR if it targets Europeans, and a good habit helps everywhere: the habits below make you compliant with the strictest law you are likely to meet, and cost almost nothing on a small form.

## Three questions for every field

Article 5 of the GDPR lists the principles: lawful, fair and transparent; limited to specified purposes; no more data than needed (data minimization); accurate; kept no longer than necessary; kept secure; and the controller can show that it complies (accountability). You laid the purpose and the retention into your database schema in [the lesson on privacy by design](../../07-data/07-privacy-by-design/); the law adds the lawful basis. For a form this turns into three questions about **each** field:

1. **Why?** The purpose is named in advance and specifically. “Call back about the inquiry” is a purpose; “for marketing” is not. What the purpose does not need is not collected: a date of birth “just in case” is not a purpose.
2. **On what basis?** Article 6(1) lists six lawful bases. For a small site, four matter:
   - **consent** of the person for one or more specific purposes;
   - **contract**: processing necessary to perform a contract with the person, or to take steps at their request before entering into one;
   - **legal obligation**: for example, tax and accounting records;
   - **legitimate interests** of the controller or a third party, unless the interests or fundamental rights of the person override them (a balance you must be able to explain).

   The other two are vital interests and public task. Consent is not the default. For an inquiry form the “steps at the request of the person before a contract” basis or legitimate interests often fit and are honest, and consent is needed where the law demands it (a newsletter, non-essential cookies: see the next lessons). In practice, pick the basis that is true, because the person can withdraw consent at any time, and then you must stop.
3. **How long?** The period is written as a number with a condition for deletion (“three years after the last contact, then I delete”). Data with no period piles up forever.

When you have these three answers for every field, you have a **record**. From it come the privacy notice, the consent text and the answer to a lawyer.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: which three questions do you ask of every form field?</summary>

Why you want it (the purpose), on what basis (consent, contract, legal obligation, legitimate interests) and how long you keep it (the period and the deletion).

</details>

## Consent

When you do rely on consent, the GDPR defines it (Article 4(11)) as a **freely given, specific, informed and unambiguous** indication of the person’s wishes, by a statement or a clear affirmative action. Article 7 adds conditions. For a form this means:

- the person **takes the action**: they tick a box that starts empty. A pre-ticked box, “by pressing the button you agree,” and silence are not consent (Recital 32 says so about pre-ticked boxes and silence);
- they see **what exactly** they agree to: who the controller is, which data, for what;
- you must be able to **demonstrate** that consent was given (Article 7(1)): the date, the version of the text, the purpose. You did this in the privacy by design lesson by recording consent as a fact;
- a request for consent made inside a longer text must be **clearly distinguishable** from the other matters, in plain language (Article 7(2)). Do not bury it inside the terms of sale;
- consent can be **withdrawn** at any time, and it must be **as easy to withdraw as to give** (Article 7(3));
- it is not “freely given” if you make a service depend on consent that it doesn’t need;
- a **newsletter** is a separate purpose and its own consent (you saw the email rules in [the email lesson](../../13-growth/02-email/); the next lessons cover cookies and ePrivacy).

How to say all this in plain words in a form, you practiced in [the lesson on legal and expert texts](../../10-writing/06-legal-expert/). How to build it in markup, you do in the next lesson.

## People’s rights

Under the GDPR a person has the right to be informed (Articles 13 and 14), to access their data (15), to have it corrected (16), erased (17), restricted (18), to take it with them (portability, 20), to object (21) and to withdraw consent (7(3)); and to complain to the supervisory authority and go to court. A request must be answered **without undue delay and in any event within one month**; the period may be extended by two further months where necessary, with notice to the person within the first month (Article 12(3)). It is normally free (Article 12(5)). The UK GDPR keeps the same rights and times. In California, under the CCPA a consumer has the right to know, delete and correct, to opt out of the sale or sharing of personal information, to limit the use of sensitive personal information and to non-discrimination. A business confirms a request within 10 business days and responds within 45 calendar days, which it may extend by another 45 days with notice.

So the privacy notice must give an address to write to, and you need a process: who reads that mailbox, and what is done on a request to delete (including copies and logs, as in the privacy by design lesson).

## What the controller must do

The main duties of the owner of a site with a form. Each one comes with its place in the law, so you can find and read it.

- **A privacy notice.** When you collect data, tell the person who you are, why you process the data, on what basis, who receives it, how long you keep it, what their rights are and how to complain (Article 13). How to write it, in the next lesson.
- **Contracts with processors** (Article 28). The processing by a processor is governed by a contract that says, among other things, that the processor acts only on your documented instructions, keeps the data secure, uses sub-processors only on conditions, helps with people’s requests and deletes or returns the data at the end. Most form services, email providers and analytics tools offer standard terms (a “data processing addendum”) to sign or accept.
- **Records of processing** (Article 30). Organizations with fewer than 250 people are exempt from keeping formal records unless the processing is likely to risk people’s rights, is not occasional, or includes special category data. A site that regularly receives inquiries is “not occasional,” so in practice write the record anyway. It is the table you build in the practice below.
- **Security** (Article 32): measures proportionate to the risk. For a small site these are what you did in [the lesson on site security](../05-security/) and [the lesson on form security](../06-forms-spam/): updates, passwords, HTTPS, input checks, access by role.
- **Special category data** (Article 9): health, political or religious views, trade union membership, sexual orientation, biometric and genetic data. Processing it needs an additional condition. If your form doesn’t ask for it but a person writes it in a free field, that is your problem: hint that extras should not be written and delete what arrives. For lawyers and expert witnesses, professional confidentiality adds to this (and in the US, an attorney’s duty of confidentiality, for example ABA Model Rule 1.6, is stricter than the privacy law).
- **Transfers outside the UK or EEA** (Chapter V, Articles 44 to 49). Personal data may leave only under a mechanism: an adequacy decision for the country (Article 45), appropriate safeguards such as the standard contractual clauses (Article 46), or a narrow exception. The EU–US Data Privacy Framework, adopted in July 2023, lets data go to certified US companies; the General Court upheld it in September 2025 and an appeal is pending, so check the current position. The UK has equivalent tools (the International Data Transfer Agreement and Addendum, and the “UK–US data bridge”).
- **Registration.** The GDPR removed the general duty to notify a regulator before processing. In the UK, however, most organizations that use personal data must pay the ICO a **data protection fee** each year unless exempt, and an organization outside the EU that falls under Article 3(2) may need to appoint a representative in the EU (Article 27). The US has no federal registration, though a few states ask data brokers to register.
- **Breaches** (Articles 33 and 34). A personal data breach must be reported to the supervisory authority **without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours** after you became aware of it, unless it is unlikely to result in a risk to people. If the risk is high, you must also tell the people. Every breach is documented (Article 33(5)). In the US, all fifty states have breach notification laws with their own deadlines and triggers.

Open the diagram, tick what your site has, and see which duties follow.

