## Fines and enforcement

The GDPR sets two tiers of administrative fines (Article 83): up to 10 million euros or 2 percent of worldwide annual turnover, and up to 20 million euros or 4 percent, whichever is higher. The UK GDPR has £8.7 million or 2 percent, and £17.5 million or 4 percent (section 157 of the Data Protection Act 2018). In California the CCPA allows administrative fines of up to $2,663 per violation and $7,988 per intentional violation or one involving consumers the violator knows are under 16 (the amounts as adjusted from 1 January 2025). Regulators fine small organizations too, but the largest fines go to the biggest ones, and a complaint from a single person is how most cases begin. These are maximums, not a price list.

## Russia, for comparison

This textbook began in Russia, and it is useful to see how the same questions are answered there. Russia’s Federal Law No. 152-FZ “On Personal Data” also defines personal data, an “operator” (the counterpart of a controller), the lawful bases and consent. Three of its rules are different in kind from the GDPR’s. First, the operator generally notifies the regulator, Roskomnadzor, before processing. Second, when collecting the personal data of Russian citizens, the recording, accumulation and storage of the primary data must be done in databases located in Russia (Article 18, part 5): that is **data localization**, the strictest answer to the question of transfers, where the GDPR instead allows transfers under conditions. Third, the deadlines for a leak are shorter: the operator reports to Roskomnadzor within 24 hours and sends the results of an internal investigation within 72 hours. These details come from the Russian edition of this lesson and change often, so for a Russian audience read the current law.

## What changed lately

Text on the internet goes out of date faster than the law. Some things to know on 30 October 2026:

- **UK:** the Data (Use and Access) Act 2025 amended the UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018, and its main data protection provisions were brought into force in early 2026. Check the ICO’s current guidance.
- **EU–US transfers:** the Data Privacy Framework stands for now, with an appeal pending before the Court of Justice.
- **United States:** more states keep passing privacy laws, and California’s regulations on automated decision-making and risk assessments are phasing in.

## For an expert’s site

An expert’s site (a lawyer, an expert witness, a surveyor) receives in its form more than a name and a phone number: people often describe their case. It can include health, family matters, criminal history and information about other people who have not agreed to anything. What to do: **say in a hint that details are not needed**, collect only what is needed for the first contact, continue the conversation through a secure channel, and remember professional confidentiality. A lawyer’s duty of confidentiality is stricter than data protection law, and a message sent through a website form may not be covered by privilege until a client relationship exists.

## Practice

You are in the era of 2022: the site stores inquiries and connects services. Draw up the record of processing for your project’s form: the controller, the source, the purposes, bases and retention periods of each field, where the data is stored, the services that receive it, special category data, and your decision on registration and fees.

The check reads the structure: that every cell is filled in, that the periods have numbers, that storage names a country or region, and that there are at least two services. It cannot judge whether your decisions are right. As in the earlier lessons, show the final version to a lawyer.

## What used to be different

Europe’s first data protection law, the Directive of 1995, left each country to write its own, and the GDPR (adopted in 2016, applying since 25 May 2018) replaced it with one regulation. Before that, many sites collected data with no policy and no consent, and inquiries piled up in the owner’s personal mailbox. In the United States the story went another way: California passed the CCPA in 2018 (in force in 2020, extended by the CPRA in 2023), and other states followed one at a time. The practice today is to treat data as someone else’s property kept in trust: write down why you took it, don’t take extra, and return or destroy it on time.
